  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1..The potential remains for some strong-severe storms, \nas well as heavy rainfall and localized flooding, mainly this \nevening into early tonight. \n\nQuite the interesting sfc analysis across the local region this \nafternoon. The highest dew points (mid-upper 70s) are situated in \nthe Piedmont with the lowest (mid-upper 60s) closer to the coast. \nUnsurprisingly, a north-south oriented warm front bisects our CWA and \nis located just E of I-95 as of 3 PM. Light showers are \noccuring across the Northern Neck and Eastern Shore with mainly \ndry conditions elsewhere. Upstream convection is located well NW\nof the area (across eastern OH and WV). \n\nThe widespread clouds and (light) precip across the area have \nintroduced some uncertainty into the storm potential, particularly \nfor this afternoon. Even W of the warm front, satellite imagery is \nshowing quite a bit of clouds. Still, mesoanalysis indicates gradual \ndestabilization is underway with 500-1500 J/kg of MLCAPE across our \nfar western counties. Negligible CAPE is present E of I-95, but this \nshould gradually chance as we head into the evening. It now seems \nlikely that the main show today will be in relation to the \naforementioned upstream convection. This activity may not push into \nthe local area until well into the evening hours and the first part \nof tonight, with a relative lull until then. This later timing also \nintroduces uncertainty into the severe wx potential. Most models \nstill do show relatively impressive shear (for mid-August) of ~30-35 \nkt this evening and tonight and this will overlap with height falls \nfrom a quick-moving shortwave trough. So, some severe risk is still \napparent from any cluster or line of storms that can move into our \narea. The northern half of the area is most favored for both the \nhighest coverage and severe potential, but some risk could extend \nsouthward as well. The main risk would be damaging wind gusts, but a \nfew instances of large hail are possible due to the favorable NW \nflow regime and supportive shear values. MCV- or warm front-\naugmented low-level shear could also support a low-end tornado \nthreat. \n\nAside from the severe threat, heavy rainfall is also a concern and \nslow-moving or back-building storms could result in scattered \ninstances of flash flooding. WPC maintains a Slight Risk ERO \ncovering much of central and eastern VA, mainly north of US-460 and \nwest of the Chesapeake Bay.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid with afternoon storms Monday ahead of \nthe next cold front. A Heat Advisory is in effect for most of NE\nNC.\n\nHot, humid conditions look to peak on Monday ahead of an \napproaching cold front, with highs reaching the low to mid 90s. \nCombined with dew points in the mid-upper 70s, heat indices of \n105+ are likely for NE NC and potentially SE VA. Have issued a \nHeat Advisory for most of our NE NC counties (minus Northampton \nNC) where confidence is highest in reaching 105+, in effect from\n11 AM-8 PM. Later shifts may need to expand the advisory \nfurther northward into SE VA if it widespread 105+ indices seem\nprobable. Some scattered showers and storms are also expected \nMonday afternoon/evening along the lingering frontal boundary \nand localized severe wind and hail is possible in the strongest \nstorms, mainly for western/SW portions of the local area. Drier \nand slightly cooler conditions arrive for Tuesday through \nmidweek. A stronger cold front looks the cross the area at the \nend of week, potentially bringing a more noticeable reduction in\nheat and humidity.",
